  "tour": {
    "title": "Romans Before You Read: Paul’s Argument, Audience, and Map",
    "intro": "Before you start, this tour gives you a map rather than a substitute for reading: first the Roman setting and Paul’s purpose, then his thesis and movement through chapters 1–8, the Israel question in 9–11, and the communal shape of 12–16. Listen for the repeated issue beneath the theology: how Jews and Gentiles become one people in the Messiah.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:63bf2f5f-com-6cd6a9f3-108c-38ad-b4b8-02550ba51633",
        "startSec": 900,
        "endSec": 1150,
        "why": "Start with the letter’s social location. The speaker notes a “fairly large Gentile component,” then pulls the situation together: Paul wants to “use the church at Rome as a base of operations” and writes so the churches will be “more united together as a church.” That answers why Romans is addressed to this particular congregation and why unity matters from the outset.",
        "episodeTitle": "Backstory to Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/i5pj3w/backstory.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1197,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:d09c8ed4-f6cf-11ee-8072-b7002c006746",
        "startSec": 230,
        "endSec": 620,
        "why": "Now meet Paul’s rhetorical posture. The conversation says he is “trying to commend himself to this church that he’s never met before,” explains his apostleship to the Gentiles, and notes “some tension between Jews and Gentiles in Rome.” Hear Romans as an apostolic defense and pastoral appeal to a real mixed community, not as an abstract theological essay.",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans Overview: Jesus is King",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG3092543348.mp3?updated=1712708592",
        "durationSec": 2862,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6bbfa166-com-20db375c-a035-35f2-8b38-516fd9b58eb7",
        "startSec": 40,
        "endSec": 430,
        "why": "This is the main chapter map. The teacher treats Romans 1:16–17 as the thesis and says “the whole rest of the letter” is an explanation of that sentence, then walks through chapters 1–4, Abraham, and chapters 5–8, where “through Christ and through the Spirit” Paul addresses sin, death, and condemnation. Keep this spine beside you as you read.",
        "episodeTitle": "An Overview of the Letter to the Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/x82wrk/overview.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1242,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:dc431d46-190e-11ef-9218-8bc4e318e54d",
        "startSec": 844,
        "endSec": 1410,
        "why": "Give chapters 9–11 their own stop rather than treating them as a detour. The segment asks, “So has God rejected his people in 11, verse 1?” and follows the answer through the remnant, mercy, and the family-tree image, including Gentiles and continuing hope for Israel. It shows why Israel’s place is part of Paul’s central question about God’s faithfulness.",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans Overview: What Happens to Israel?",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG3311501314.mp3?updated=1716474009",
        "durationSec": 2673,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6bbfa166-com-20db375c-a035-35f2-8b38-516fd9b58eb7",
        "startSec": 679,
        "endSec": 875,
        "why": "Finish with the “therefore.” The overview says that the argument of chapters 1–11 has been leading toward how God’s people live together, calls chapter 14 “the sharpest point” of that issue, and notes the concluding greetings in chapter 16. This lets you hear chapters 12–16 as the embodied, communal outcome of the gospel argument rather than an unrelated appendix.",
        "episodeTitle": "An Overview of the Letter to the Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/x82wrk/overview.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1242,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "As you read, keep three questions in view: What verdict is Paul leveling at both Jew and Gentile? How does Jesus and the Spirit answer the problem he has exposed? And what kind of shared life should follow? Those questions will help the letter’s long argument, its Israel section, and its practical closing read as one connected movement.",
    "totalSeconds": 1792
  },
